<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:system="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!--
        The only file in this product allowed to hold an appearance value.

        ADR-23. Everything else refers to these by name, and a guard in the architecture
        tests fails the build on a value written into a view. The reason is not tidiness:
        XAML is prose, a margin of seven instead of eight breaks nothing, and that is exactly
        how an interface drifts into looking like two products.

        Reading this file top to bottom is meant to be the same thing as knowing what the
        product looks like. If that stops being true, this file has grown wrong.

        The base look comes from the built-in Fluent theme in dark mode (ADR-24), so what
        lives here is only what is ours: the spacing scale, type sizes, and the colours that
        mean something about an entry rather than merely decorating it.
    -->

    <!--
        The spacing scale. Closed on purpose.

        A session that wants seven cannot have seven - it picks the nearest step. That is the
        whole mechanism against "six here, eight there", and it only works because the guard
        makes the alternative fail the build rather than merely being discouraged.

        Four steps, doubling. More steps means more ways for two screens to differ while both
        being "correct".
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="SpaceTight">4</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="SpaceNormal">8</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="SpaceWide">16</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="SpaceSection">24</system:Double>

    <system:Double x:Key="SizeNone">0</system:Double>

    <Thickness x:Key="PadCell">8,4,8,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        The list cell, and it is deliberately not PadCell.

        Measured on screen 2026-08-02: WPF UI's cell template honours TemplateBinding Padding
        and the built-in one did not, so the same 8,4,8,4 that was invisible before the
        migration became 6.6 logical pixels of row height after it - 29 rows on screen fell to
        22. The owner chose to keep this list's density, so the vertical half goes to nothing
        and row height is decided by the line height alone.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadListCell">8,0,8,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PadPanel">16</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginBetweenControls">0,0,8,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginNone">0</Thickness>

    <!--
        Type. Three sizes, because a fourth would need a reason nobody has yet.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeNormal">13</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeHeading">18</system:Double>
    <system:Double x:Key="TextSizeSmall">11</system:Double>

    <FontFamily x:Key="TextFace">Segoe UI</FontFamily>

    <!--
        Monospaced, and only where alignment carries meaning: a launch path, a hash, a
        security descriptor. Reading a hash in a proportional face is how two different
        hashes look the same.
    -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="TextFaceFixed">Consolas</FontFamily>

    <!--
        Colours that say something.

        Deliberately not a palette. The controls take their look from the Fluent dark theme,
        so what is defined here is the small set that makes a claim about an entry - and each
        one is named after what it means rather than after what it looks like. A brush called
        "Red" gets used for the next red thing somebody needs. A brush called "Untrusted"
        does not.
    -->
    <!--
        EVERY COLOUR BELOW IS CHOSEN BY CALCULATION, NOT BY EYE, and a test enforces it -
        ContrastGuards reads this file, resolves the background out of WPF UI's own dictionary
        and fails the build below the threshold for the brush's role. Adding a brush here
        without giving it a role fails too, so the guard cannot quietly miss one.

        It exists because five of ten brushes did not pass when somebody finally counted, on
        2026-08-02. The worst was this one: #E81123 gave 3.52 against 4.5 required, and it
        carries the two most important sentences in the tool - an untrusted signature and a
        query with a mistake in it. The least readable colour on the least forgiving message.

        tools/contrast/contrast.ps1 is the instrument for choosing a replacement. WCAG 2.2
        SC 1.4.3 for text, SC 1.4.11 for surfaces.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MeaningUntrusted">#F1707A</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MeaningWarning">#FFB900</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MeaningRunning">#6CCB5F</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        What the person wrote and we cannot read - a query with a mistake in it.

        Its own name rather than the untrusted one, even though the colour is the same today.
        A brush borrowed for a second meaning is how the next session ends up changing the
        colour of a rejected query by adjusting what an untrusted signature looks like.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MeaningRejected">#F1707A</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        What nobody could read. Its own colour because it is its own state: this project
        spends most of its rules on not letting "unknown" look like "nothing", and the
        interface is the last place that could undo all of it.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MeaningUnknown">#9E9E9E</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        Text that is present but subordinate - a display name beside a service name, a count
        beside a group. Not a colour decision so much as a hierarchy one.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextSubdued">#A0A0A0</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        Selection, and it is here because the built-in theme does not cover it.

        Measured by looking: with ThemeMode="Dark" the Fluent theme styles the controls but
        leaves DataGrid's own selection alone, so a selected row came out light grey with
        dark text on a dark window - and the focused cell drew a box that reads as somewhere
        to type, on a list that is read only. That is the "two products in one window" this
        whole file exists to prevent, arriving from the one direction ADR-24 assumed was
        covered.
    -->
    <!--
        Selection has to be TELLABLE APART from an ordinary row, which is what WCAG 2.2
        SC 1.4.11 asks of a component state, so it answers to 3:1 against the list background
        and not to taste. The old #2D4F6C gave 1.90.

        Two ratios pull against each other here and both are checked: lighten the surface and
        it separates better from the background while white text on it separates worse.
        #3F7099 gives 3.09 against the background and 5.26 for white on top - the only pair in
        the search where both clear their threshold with room.

        Hover answers to a floor of OUR choosing and that is said plainly rather than dressed
        up as a standard: 1.4.11 covers states needed to IDENTIFY a component, and a pointer
        already says where the pointer is. What is not defensible is 1.14, which is what
        #2A2A2A gave - below the point where the state can be seen at all.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceSelected">#3F7099</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceHover">#454545</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextOnSurface">#FFFFFF</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        A row that moved a moment ago. A10 rule three: a change has to be visible rather than
        stealthy, because a list that quietly corrects itself is one where somebody looking
        away misses the thing they were waiting for.

        Deliberately dim. It marks every service that starts or stops while the window is
        open, which on a working machine is a steady trickle - loud enough to catch the eye
        from across the screen and it would be a fairground.

        DIM IS NOT THE SAME AS INVISIBLE, and #3A3320 was the second. It measured 1.30 against
        the background, and that number explains an argument this project could not settle for
        three slices: whether the colour was reaching a pixel at all. At 1.30 it hardly shows
        when it does arrive, so a rendering fault and a contrast fault looked identical from
        the outside. #63563A keeps the amber and measures 2.27.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceChanged">#63563A</SolidColorBrush>

    <Thickness x:Key="MarginAboveStatus">0,8,0,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginBelowSearch">0,0,0,8</Thickness>

    <!--
        Default look per control type, so a control is consistent without anybody remembering
        to apply a style. The guard checks these too: every Value below points at a name.
    -->
    <!--
        THE THREE ROW STATES LIVE ON THE ROW, AND THAT MOVED HERE FROM THE CELL ON 2026-08-02.
        Read the next four paragraphs before touching either style - this exact question has
        now been got wrong twice, in opposite directions, and both times silently.

        Under the built-in Fluent theme the cell painted an OPAQUE background and covered the
        row, so a Background setter on DataGridRow reached nothing. Three states sat there,
        exactly one appeared, and it only appeared because the same brush was also on the cell.
        Fourteen frames of a service stopping, and the declared #3A3320 never showed once.

        Under WPF UI it is the other way round, and this is read out of their markup rather
        than guessed: DefaultDataGridRowStyle templates its border as
        Background="{TemplateBinding Background}", while DefaultDataGridCellStyle hardcodes
        Background="Transparent" on its own border and never binds the property at all. So a
        Background setter on the CELL is now the dead one.

        WPF UI supplies no feedback of its own to lean on. Every VisualState in their row and
        cell templates - Normal_Selected, MouseOver, all of them - is present and EMPTY, with
        the storyboards commented out in their source. Selection and hover are ours to draw,
        the same as before.

        RowStyle and CellStyle are set explicitly on the DataGrid in MainWindow.xaml rather
        than left to implicit lookup. Their DefaultDataGridStyle sets both properties itself,
        and an explicit setter on the grid beats an implicit style - so leaving these keyed but
        unreferenced would be a third way to make the same mistake.

        tools/gui-probe/check.ps1 is the only instrument that can tell whether any of this
        reaches a pixel. A property read says #3A3320 either way.
    -->
    <!--
        The grid itself, and it exists for one setter that is not optional.

        DataGridTextColumn TRANSFERS FontSize from the grid onto every TextBlock it generates,
        as a LOCAL value - and a local value beats a default style. So the implicit TextBlock
        style below, which carries our size, loses inside every cell of this list. Their
        DefaultDataGridStyle sets FontSize to their own DefaultDataGridFontSize, and the first
        build after the migration came out with rows a third taller and the PID column clipped
        to two digits, because the wider text pushed the automatic columns past the viewport.

        Neither symptom looked like a font size, and that is why this comment is here.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListGrid" TargetType="DataGrid" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="ListRow" TargetType="DataGridRow" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridRowStyle}">
        <!--
            Margin and padding pulled back to nothing, against their 0,2 and 4. The owner chose
            to keep this list's density on 2026-08-02: their spacing costs about 17% of the
            rows on screen, measured twice, and this window exists to show 810 services.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginNone}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource MarginNone}" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <!--
                First, so that pointing at a row or selecting it still wins. A person's own
                hand beats a notification about something that already happened.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding RecentlyChanged}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceChanged}" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
            </Trigger>

            <!--
                LAST, so selection beats the pointer, and that order is measured rather than
                assumed. With the pointer winning, a row went grey the instant it was clicked
                and stayed grey for as long as the hand did not move - so the one row the
                person had just chosen was the one row that did not look chosen.
            -->
            <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
            </Trigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The cell carries spacing and nothing else. Any Background set here is discarded by
        their template - see the block above.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListCell" TargetType="DataGridCell" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridCellStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadListCell}" />
        <!--
            Against their MinHeight of 32. Row height is decided by the padding above, which is
            the same decision as the margins on the row: keep the density this list had.
        -->
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource SizeNone}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        BasedOn by NAME on WPF UI's own styles, not on {x:Type TextBlock}.

        The type form worked while the base was the built-in Fluent theme, and would now be
        ambiguous: this dictionary is merged after theirs and is itself keyed by the same type,
        so which style it resolves to depends on lookup order rather than on intent. Their keys
        are stable, documented by their own markup - DataGrid.xaml bases four styles on exactly
        these names - and they say what is meant.

        CONSEQUENCE TO KNOW: this file no longer loads on its own. It resolves keys that only
        exist once ControlsDictionary is merged, which is why tools/wpfui-probe loads it after
        theirs. That is a real dependency and it is the shape of the decision the owner made.

        Every one of the three below keeps OUR size setter on top of their template. That is
        the density decision of 2026-08-02 in the two places it is spelled out.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="HeadingText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="FixedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SubduedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        What the result has to admit about itself, and what is wrong with the question. Two
        styles rather than one, because they answer different questions and a person glancing
        at the window has to be able to tell "your query is broken" from "your query worked and
        the answer has holes in it" without reading either sentence.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="NoticeText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="ProblemText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The one field. Its padding is named rather than left to the framework because the hint
        sitting behind it has to line up with the text a person types, and the only way to be
        sure of that is for both to come from this line.

        BasedOn is the whole reason this comment is long. Without it a default style here does
        not ADD to the base one, it REPLACES it - so the field came out white, with grey hint
        text, on a dark window. It shipped that way from S6a until 2026-08-02, through a
        screenshot review that had the field on screen.

        Found by accident, while photographing this window to compare it against a control
        library. That is the fourth time in this project that XAML has bound correctly and
        painted something other than what it promised, after the empty list from internal view
        models, the column headers outside the visual tree, and the dead DataGridRow background.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultTextBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="CheckBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultCheckBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
